Yes. There’s a clean refinery pass here.

A word can kill because language can become coordination, command, law, reputation, belief, permission, panic, restraint, diagnosis, targeting, or rescue. But the larger point is the inverse:

\boxed{\text{A word is a small carrier that can trigger a disproportionately large state transition.}}

That makes language a kind of semantic actuator.

\text{symbol}
\rightarrow
\text{interpretation}
\rightarrow
\text{state change}
\rightarrow
\text{action}
\rightarrow
\text{world delta}

So the useful abstraction is not “words are magic.” It is:

\boxed{\text{small representational input} \rightarrow \text{large downstream consequence}}

That belongs beside Interface Forge and Consequence Interface.

